WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.283 --> 00:00:01.150
(suspenseful music)

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:03.950
- [Narrator] This is a premium
Wagyu steak from Japan,

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:07.183
and costs around $100 at
supermarkets in the US.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.641
And this is also Wagyu, but it costs $40.

00:00:12.641 --> 00:00:15.090
(upbeat music)

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:16.810
That's because it comes from here,

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:18.530
the vast country of Australia,

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:21.050
where ranchers are racing
ahead to win the global market

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.800
for Wagyu by experimenting
with different methods

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:25.250
to raise the cattle.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.320
- We're running about 2000 Wagyu cattle,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.280
the Wagyus have been the linchpin

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.150
for our growth as a business.

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.280
- [Narrator] All this doesn't sit well

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.241
with Japanese farmers.

00:00:35.241 --> 00:00:38.991
(speaks in foreign language)

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.630
Japan and Australia are
the biggest Wagyu producers

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:49.610
in the world.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.730
And they're vying for
the largest export market

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.570
that is in US.

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:57.000
By 2023, the global Wagyu
industry will generate sales

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.370
of around $9.5 billion,

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:01.770
according to the marker
research from Technavio.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.540
So Australian and Japanese farmers

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.530
are in an intense competition

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.808
with one country betting on tradition.

00:01:08.808 --> 00:01:12.558
(speaks in foreign language)

00:01:15.578 --> 00:01:17.540
While the other is
betting on new technology

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.650
to increase productivity.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.950
- We are mapping the entire genome,

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:24.080
I'll know whether to breed on from it,

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.050
or it's gonna end up on a dinner plate.

00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:28.120
So this is really powerful technology

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:29.977
and we're using it to full effect.

00:01:31.785 --> 00:01:34.260
- [Narrator] Wagyu is a breed
of cattle native to Japan,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.023
and literally translates
into Japanese beef.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.030
What makes it so special is
the high concentration of fat

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.630
that's inside the muscles.

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:44.503
The industry calls it marbling.

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.297
And that's what farmer
Kazuki Morimoto does best.

00:01:48.297 --> 00:01:52.047
(speaks in foreign language)

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:55.510
He says the trick to his success

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.579
has been following strict traditions.

00:01:57.579 --> 00:02:01.329
(speaks in foreign language)

00:02:05.145 --> 00:02:09.030
Wagyu farms are usually
small, but Morimoto's 300 cows

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:13.333
have brought in $1.4 million
annually in the past few years.

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.290
A small herd allows them
to pay very close attention

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:22.240
to the feed, which accounts
for almost 70% of his costs.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.990
(speaks in foreign language)

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:32.690
Morimoto usually fattens
cows for a minimum

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.370
of two-and-a-half years,

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:36.780
compared to the average
one-and-a-half years it takes

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.060
for other types of cattle like Angus.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:41.030
He says his Wagyu is meant to be consumed

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:44.570
in small quantities, similar
to other high-end delicacies

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:47.004
like caviar and Foie gras.

00:02:47.004 --> 00:02:50.754
(speaks in foreign language)

00:02:55.462 --> 00:02:56.730
(birds chirping)

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:59.210
Many Australian ranchers
see the Japanese way

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.760
of raising Wagyu as out-of-date.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.490
- As the Japanese, they're
feeding the animals

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:05.900
basically from birth.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.230
They just feed feed feed.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.120
And we're not doing that.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:12.250
- [Narrator] Steve Binnie is part

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.670
of a new generation of Wagyu ranchers,

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:18.500
who want to produce beef in
what he says is the modern way.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.190
He had his first Wagyu
steak five years ago.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.067
- Some people say, "Where were you when,

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:24.487
"you know September 11 happened?

00:03:24.487 --> 00:03:26.700
"Where were you when Kennedy got shot?"

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.000
And so I look at this moment in my life

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.470
was when I had that first
Wagyu, and it was so incredible.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.810
- [Narrator] The next day, Binnie pivoted

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.130
his nearly 100-year-old family business

00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:40.000
from Hereford cattle to Wagyu.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.320
- So this is an eight to nine strip line.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:44.710
Beautiful, this is what
we'll be having for lunch.

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:46.540
The Australian product does cost less

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.470
because we're more efficient.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:52.723
We're doing it on large-scale
with low cost of production.

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:56.420
- [Narrator] Australian ranchers
have perfected a technology

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.690
that Japanese farmers like Morimoto

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:00.620
won't ever consider using.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.940
Agricultural genomics, it's
the analysis and selection

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:06.910
of the best DNA in Wagyu cattle.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:09.380
Then he has been artificially
inseminating his cows

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:11.410
since he started raising Wagyu.

00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:13.680
For this cow, he's
carefully selected sperm

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.270
so that the resulting calf

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.660
will thrive in the hot Australian climate,

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.700
need less water and food
compared to a cow in Japan,

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:24.320
but still grow up to become
Wagyu that's rich in fat.

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:26.100
- It's a very data-driven process.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.700
All of the carcasses that are
coming through the abattoir,

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:31.520
they are evaluated for
a matter of marbling,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.350
weight, age, days on
feed, all of this data

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.230
is going into this monstrous database

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.830
and all of these carcasses
are being linked back

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.440
to the genetics.

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.390
- [Narrator] Using this,
Australian ranchers

00:04:46.390 --> 00:04:49.760
also create cross-breeds
with other cows like Angus,

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:51.920
these steaks usually have less marbling

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.580
because they're genetically 50% Wagyu.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:57.685
So about half the price of
full-blood Wagyu steaks.

00:04:57.685 --> 00:05:00.310
(steak sizzling)

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:02.350
Many of these cross-bred Wagyu steaks,

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.130
end up on plates in the US,

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:06.880
where local Wagyu production
makes up a tiny portion

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:08.633
of the larger cattle industry.

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:13.120
By 2021, Binnie expects to
increase the sales to the US

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:15.033
by up to 50%.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:18.543
- So good.

00:05:19.447 --> 00:05:21.650
- [Narrator] Wagyu embryos
first arrived in Australia

00:05:21.650 --> 00:05:23.670
in the early 1990s.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.230
But the combination of genomics

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:28.660
and Australia's ability to
scale cheaper Wagyu production

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:30.550
has led to a boom in demand,

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.420
helping the Australian industry grow

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.730
by 20% every year since 2015.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:38.450
Last year, the country sold 40,000 tons

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:39.820
of Wagyu beef abroad,

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.980
making it the largest
exporter of the steaks

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:43.770
on the planet by volume,

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:47.851
well ahead of Japan's
export of just 4300 times.

00:05:47.851 --> 00:05:50.500
(speaks in foreign language)
(sound indicator beeping)

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.350
Farmers like Morimoto have been feeling

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:53.980
the market gets sucked away from them

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:55.710
by Australian ranchers.

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:57.660
So the Japanese government has stepped in

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:00.402
to protect and promote its Wagyu industry.

00:06:00.402 --> 00:06:05.402
(speaks in foreign language)
(sound indicator beeping)

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:08.900
Auction houses like this
one are the only places

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:12.150
where Japanese farmers
can sell their Wagyu beef.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:15.350
For years, these places were
not allowed to sell to the US,

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:17.710
a coveted market for Wagyu farmers.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.790
The last year Japan signed
a key trade deal with the US

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.110
to export unlimited Wagyu,

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:25.860
a key part of the Japanese
government's grand strategy

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.394
to double beef exports.

00:06:28.394 --> 00:06:31.300
(upbeat music)

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.140
The government also recently passed a bill

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:36.449
that criminalizes the export
of Wagyu's semen and embryos.

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:38.230
(cameras clicking)

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:41.250
But experts say these reforms
don't really get up the heart

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.590
of the issue with Japan's industry.

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.830
- It's a core deal,
what Wagyu original beef

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:51.830
is a very excellent but
price is too expensive.

00:06:52.354 --> 00:06:54.190
- [Narrator] Takafumi
Gotoh is an agronomist

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:56.420
who studies the Wagyu beef industry.

00:06:56.420 --> 00:06:59.320
He says that in order for
Japanese farmers to catch up,

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.660
they need to start copying

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.870
what Australian ranchers are doing;

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.440
produce Wagyu beef that's less rich in fat

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.923
and therefore cheaper.

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.940
- Australian farmers
consider as a business.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.050
But the Japanese farmers,
the most important thing

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.810
is to produce a good marbling beef.

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:19.156
Japanese farmer should do a business,

00:07:19.156 --> 00:07:22.100
consider the cost of
performance, how much feed?

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:22.973
How much profit?

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:25.930
- [Narrator] But that won't
be something Japanese ranchers

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:27.888
will accept easily.

00:07:27.888 --> 00:07:31.638
(speaks in foreign language)

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:50.270
- This is an A4 Miyazaki
ribeye from Japan.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:53.593
And here we have a
beautiful Australian ribeye.

